            AwakeningLesson(
                id: "joa_m12_l2",
                number: 2,
                title: "The 8 Indicators",
                subtitle: "Score each 0-12.5. Total = manipulation likelihood.",
                emoji: "📊",
                sections: [
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Indicator 1: Focus Hijack (0-12.5)", body: "WHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nRepetition, shocking visuals, disproportionate airtime, \"breaking news\" loops, algorithmic amplification.\n\nThe first move in any psyop is capturing attention. If every channel, every feed, every conversation is dominated by ONE story — ask why. Reality is complex. When it suddenly simplifies to a single narrative, someone is controlling the lens.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Balanced, proportional coverage across multiple stories\n3 = Slightly elevated attention but organic (genuine major event)\n6 = Repetitive looping with emotional hooks\n9 = 24/7 saturation with identical framing across outlets\n12.5 = Total attention capture + active suppression of competing stories\n\nSHADOW CHECK: Is YOUR attention being hijacked because the story triggers a personal fear? Focus hijack works by exploiting existing prediction errors. If you're already anxious about X, a story about X captures you faster than someone who isn't."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Indicator 2: Authority Inversion (0-12.5)", body: "WHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nExperts speaking outside their domain, manufactured consensus, tone shifts from informing to commanding, credential-waving without evidence, \"trust the science\" from non-scientists.\n\nLegitimate authority says: \"Here's what we found, here's our methodology, here are the limitations, draw your own conclusion.\"\n\nInverted authority says: \"We're the experts. Don't question us. If you disagree, you're dangerous.\"\n\nThe difference is transparency vs. demand for compliance.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Domain-specific experts with transparent methodology and acknowledged uncertainty\n3 = Mild credential-leverage but evidence provided\n6 = Experts speaking outside domain OR consensus manufactured by repetition\n9 = Active silencing of credentialed dissenters + emotional authority appeals\n12.5 = Full authority inversion: questioning = heresy, compliance = virtue\n\nQUANTUM CHECK: Authority creates context. Your judgment about the SAME evidence changes depending on who presents it. That's contextuality. If removing the expert's name changes your assessment of the data — you're measuring the authority, not the evidence."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Indicators 3-4: Tribal Polarization & Emotion Override", body: "INDICATOR 3: TRIBAL POLARIZATION (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nUs-vs-them language, identity labels (\"patriots vs. traitors,\" \"science believers vs. deniers\"), in-group signaling, shaming for questioning, social exclusion threats.\n\nHumans are wired for tribal belonging. We NEED groups. That wiring is 200,000 years old. Every psyop exploits it by creating artificial tribes and making you afraid of being expelled from yours.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Neutral language, issue-focused, no identity attachment\n3 = Mild in-group preference but no demonization\n6 = Clear us/them framing with emotional charge\n9 = Active dehumanization of out-group + social punishment for dissent\n12.5 = Full tribal warfare narrative: loyalty = survival, questioning = betrayal\n\nUNITY CHECK: Does this narrative DIVIDE or UNITE? Every engineered narrative benefits from division. United people are ungovernable. Divided people are controllable. If the story makes you hate a group of humans — that's the tell.\n\n---\n\nINDICATOR 4: EMOTION OVERRIDE (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nFear without evidence, hope without substance, outrage without context, urgency without justification, scarcity pressure (\"act now or lose everything\").\n\nEmotions are data. They're valid. But when a narrative LEADS with emotion and provides evidence only as afterthought (or not at all) — that's emotional scripting.\n\nThe amygdala processes threats 5x faster than the prefrontal cortex processes logic. Every psyop is designed to hit the amygdala before the cortex has time to evaluate.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Data-first presentation, emotions arise naturally from facts\n3 = Some emotional framing but facts available\n6 = Emotion leads, evidence trails behind\n9 = Pure emotional scripting with minimal verifiable data\n12.5 = Deliberate fear/outrage manufacturing + scarcity pressure + \"don't think, act now\"\n\nSHADOW CHECK: Which specific emotion is being triggered? If it's fear — what shadow material does it activate? Your personal fear signature is your vulnerability. Know it, and you become harder to hijack."),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Indicators 5-6: Novelty/Timing & Context Shift", body: "INDICATOR 5: NOVELTY & TIMING EXPLOITATION (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nSudden \"breaking\" events perfectly timed to distract from something else. New crisis erupting the day a damaging report drops. Pattern of convenient coincidences.\n\nThe news cycle has a memory of about 72 hours. Anything that falls outside that window effectively disappears. Timing is the most underrated manipulation tool.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Organic timeline, no suspicious correlation with other events\n3 = Mild timing convenience but likely coincidental\n6 = Suspicious timing that warrants investigation\n9 = Clear distraction pattern: new crisis perfectly buries previous story\n12.5 = Manufactured event with precision timing + coordinated rollout across platforms\n\nPREDICTIVE CODING CHECK: Your brain's model expects \"breaking news\" to be important. That expectation is exploitable. High-novelty events generate massive prediction errors, flooding your system with attention and emotion — perfect conditions for slipping something past your rational defenses.\n\n---\n\nINDICATOR 6: CONTEXT BOUNDARY SHIFT (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\n\"Emergency\" powers that normalize extreme measures, temporary rules that become permanent, gradual expansion of acceptable control, rapid compliance pressure (\"we don't have time to debate this\").\n\nThe phrase to watch for: \"Never let a crisis go to waste.\" — Rahm Emanuel\n\nEvery engineered crisis is an opportunity to shift the boundary of what's considered normal. Once shifted, it rarely shifts back.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Consistent rules, transparent process, proportional response\n3 = Minor boundary adjustment with clear sunset clause\n6 = Significant shift framed as \"temporary\" with vague timeline\n9 = Permanent power expansion using emergency justification + social pressure to comply\n12.5 = Complete context rewrite: old normal erased, questioning the new normal = dangerous"),
                    AwakeningSection(heading: "Indicators 7-8: Follow-the-Money & Frame Analysis", body: "INDICATOR 7: FOLLOW-THE-MONEY & BENEFICIARY MAPPING (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nFunding trails, lobbying connections, industry gain from the narrative, stock movements around events, who profits from the proposed \"solution.\"\n\nThis is the most concrete indicator. Emotions can be debated. Money can be tracked.\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Transparent funding, no conflicts of interest, broad public benefit\n3 = Minor conflicts but disclosed\n6 = Undisclosed conflicts + clear financial beneficiaries\n9 = Massive profit concentration + active concealment of financial connections\n12.5 = Entire narrative exists to funnel resources to specific beneficiaries + evidence of pay-to-play\n\nACTION: Always ask \"Who benefits financially from me believing this?\" If you can't identify the beneficiary, dig deeper. If you find the beneficiary and they funded the narrative — score accordingly.\n\n---\n\nINDICATOR 8: FRAME & LOGICAL FALLACY CLUSTER (0-12.5)\n\nWHAT TO LOOK FOR:\nMicro-agreements (\"We can all agree that...\"), hero/villain archetypes (\"brave whistleblower vs. corrupt institution\" or reverse), and logical fallacies:\n\n1. Appeal to Emotion — \"Think of the children!\"\n2. Straw Man — Misrepresenting the opposing view to make it easier to attack\n3. Bandwagon — \"Everyone agrees\" / \"The science is settled\"\n4. False Dilemma — \"You're either with us or against us\"\n5. Ad Hominem — Attack the person, not the argument\n6. Appeal to Authority — \"Dr. Expert says so\" (without evidence)\n7. Slippery Slope — \"If we allow X, soon Y and Z will happen\"\n8. Hasty Generalization — \"One example proves the rule\"\n9. Red Herring — Introducing irrelevant topic to distract\n10. False Equivalence — Equating two very different things\n\nSCORING:\n0 = Clean logic, transparent framing, no fallacies detected\n3 = 1 fallacy, likely unintentional\n6 = 2-3 fallacies with clear narrative framing\n9 = 4+ fallacies + hero/villain archetype + micro-agreements\n12.5 = Full narrative engineering: layered fallacies + identity hooks + archetype casting + deliberate frame control"),
